  "worked_solution": "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{6x+1}x & <-{3}^{6x}{x}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{6x}{x}^{2}-{3}^{6x+1}x & <0 \\\\ {3}^{6x}({x}^{2}-3x) & <0 \\\\ {3}^{6x}x(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{6x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-3)<0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $0<x<3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042952/assets/8582e3a512cc29618a3e2db9dda4478481167eefcf0a2fbdfb7d33e5786ba62e.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is  $0<x<3.$",
